    "lawSection": "Correctional Services",
    "memo": " BILL NUMBER:  S5987\n\n TITLE OF BILL :  An act authorizing the study of parole officer\nstaffing; and providing for the repeal of such provisions upon\nexpiration thereof\n\n PURPOSE OR GENERAL IDEA OF BILL :\n\nTo conduct a study regarding parole officer staffing, which must\ninclude consideration of national standards, to determine adequate\nstaffing\n\n SUMMARY OF PROVISIONS :\n\nSection 1 authorizes the department of corrections and community\nsupervision to conduct a study regarding the number of parole officers\nassigned to community supervision.\n\nSection 2 requires the commissioner to provide a report to the\ngovernor and the legislature on such report along with any\nrecommendations necessary.\n\nSection 3 authorizes the commissioner to receive information from any\nstate agency necessary in order to complete this study and report.\n\nSection 4 establishes the effective date.\n\n JUSTIFICATION :\n\nThe Division of Parole, established in 1930, has undergone many\nchanges as an Executive agency. It first merged with the Department of\nCorrectional Services back in 1971 and reestablished as a stand-alone\nagency in 1978. In 2011, it was merged back into the Department which\nwas renamed the Department of Corrections and Community Supervision\n(DOCCS).\n\nSince the merging, the Legislature no longer receives reports and\ntestimony exclusive to the performance, goals and needs of parole or\ncommunity supervision. Budgetary reductions, the restructuring of what\nhad been the Division of Parole by the Department, and a significant\ndecrease in the number of parole officers, together with new statutory\nrequirements and modern practices related to post-release supervision,\ngraduated sanctions, risk and needs assessments, and caseload ratios\nby level of perceived risk make it difficult to answer the question of\nwhether or not we currently have enough parole officers to keep the\npublic safe and to help parolees successfully reintegrate into\nsociety.\n\nThis bill will help answer the question of whether we need more parole\nofficers by requiring the Commissioner of DOCCS to conduct a staffing\nstudy taking into account parole and community supervision standards\nset by the American Correctional Association, national parole\nstandards and other published best practices. The Commissioner, within\nsix months of the enactment date, must report any findings and\nrecommendations to the Governor and the Legislature.\n\n PRIOR LEGISLATION HISTORY :\n\nNew Bill\n\n FISCAL IMPLICATIONS :\n\nMinimal\n\n EFFECTIVE DATE :\nThis act shall take effect immediately and shall expire and be deemed\nrepealed thirty days after the delivery of the report to the Governor\nand the Legislature as provided for in section two of this act.\n",
